  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings and cross-platform comparison are based on a single city, Hong Kong, which may limit generalizability to cities with different urban densities or street layouts.",
      "what": "The study evaluates the consistency of physical streetscape elements and human perceptual scores between Baidu Street View and Google Street View using paired images from Hong Kong. At the sampling-point scale, vegetation, buildings, and sky show strong cross-platform correlations of 0.753, 0.756, and 0.720, while perceptual dimensions like safety and depressiveness correlate between 0.591 and 0.672. Robustness tests using SegFormer, DeepLabv3+, ResNet50, and CLIP reveal that physical structural consistency remains stable across segmentation pipelines, whereas perceptual consistency is highly sensitive to the chosen model backbone.",
      "why": "It establishes that physical streetscape metrics can be reliably integrated across different street-view platforms, though researchers must account for model backbone sensitivity when mapping subjective human perceptions."
    }
  },
